  "description": "Two fire-rating stories are recorded under the literal comparison on Monday: story-1 (\"90 min\" against \"1.5 h\") and story-5 (\"60 min\" against \"1.5 h\"). On Wednesday the owner's normalization of the project, recorded from the meeting's minutes, names the fixture fire-rating interpretation. Its write records story-1's end act at Wednesday, cause normalization, citing the normalization; story-5, whose sides still differ under the interpretation, continues and is compared under it. A Tuesday frame is not rewritten: story-1 reads open and disagreeing, the conflicts read reports the literal difference and the resolved frame names literal comparison with no normalization. A Thursday frame naming no interpretation resolves to the fire-rating interpretation and identifies the normalization; story-1 reads ended and aligned, matched by the normalization, and the conflicts read returns the pair as matched rather than as a conflict. A reader naming the interpretation is told on Tuesday that the sides match, without changing the story's standing.",
  "note": "7.3.8: \"Every story about the subject whose baseline period is open at T (7.6.3), and whose sides match under its interpretation from T (7.2.4), ends at T ... Its baseline end act has cause `normalization`, with as its record the normalization or normalizations that put that interpretation in force (7.6.5). Stories that still differ continue ... Before T the earlier interpretation governs\". 4.6.1: when the frame names none, \"the resolved frame returns the interpretation in force for the read's scope at the cutoff ... A resolved frame of this kind identifies the normalizations that put that interpretation in force, or that none applies\". 9.4.2: \"Pairs whose values differ under literal comparison but are equal under an interpretation that a normalization put in force are not conflicts; the read returns them separately, each with that normalization\". README section 5, story: match_under_read_interpretation \"is present only when the read's interpretation differs from the story's at the cutoff\". A normalization ending carries no matched_by: 7.6.5 gives it to alignment and fulfillment ends only.",
